      "analysis": "STR - Defects and Anomalies Analysis\n\nDefects and Anomalies Analysis\n\nThis section details the analysis of defects and anomalies identified during the telemetry acquisition subsystem preliminary design review.\n\nAnalysis:\n\nRequirement Discrepancies: The initial design for the acquisition subsystem did not explicitly define the sampling rate for each channel (REQ-0001). This discrepancy was addressed by Dr. Sharma's action item to update SysRS section 2 to clarify this requirement.\nREST Interface Versioning:  The REST interface in REQ-0005 is required to be versioned from day one (Decision: REQ-0005 shall be versioned). This ensures consistency and traceability of changes.\nAuthentication Mechanism Confirmation: Lt. Rao's action item requires confirmation of the authentication mechanism for the security panel with respect to REQ-0008. This will ensure secure access control.\nRequirement Modifications:  REQ-0006 was modified to require structured JSON logging output, and a new requirement (REQ-0011) was added to mandate automatic fault recovery within 30 seconds. This ensures comprehensive data logging and robust system functionality.\n\nTest Outcomes Analysis:\n\nREQ-0001: The hardware-in-loop demonstration successfully acquired all three channels. This indicates the subsystem's ability to collect telemetry data as per the defined requirements.\nREQ-0005:  The sample retrieval endpoint did not meet the latency budget during the load test, resulting in a FAIL outcome. This highlights the need for further optimization of the REST interface to ensure timely data retrieval.\nREQ-0002: The MTBF estimate recorded on the reference platform exceeded 2000 hours. This indicates the subsystem's reliability and potential for long-term operation.\n\nNext Steps:\n\nAddress the identified defects and anomalies, including the sampling rate clarification and REST interface optimization.\nImplement the automatic fault recovery mechanism as per REQ-0011.\nConduct further testing to validate the system's performance under various conditions."
    },
